many of all of our plans for  thanksgiving have changed this year  because of the coronavirus  and that means more of us could be  prepping our very first turkey day meal  this time  federal health officials want to remind  everyone about proper food safety  practices to avoid getting sick  the usda says when it's time to cook the  bird it's best to use a food thermometer  also make sure you are not  cross-contaminating wash and sanitize  all the surfaces  cutting boards utensils and your hands  wash your hands before you prepare food  wash your hands  during food preparation if you're  handling something raw  or something that could be contaminated  and wash your hands  afterward 95 to 99  of our consumers in observational  studies don't  wash your hands at all or they don't  properly wash your hands  yes we need to wash your hands you don't  want to leave the food out all day  either  get those leftovers into the fridge  within two hours and put them in a  smaller container so they chill quickly  and toss everything after four days  that's the magic number  the american farm bureau federation  reports thanksgiving dinner will cost  less this year a traditional meal for 10  people is expected to cost about 47  bucks  a person that's four percent cheaper  than last year a 16 pound turkey cost  about 20  and that is also down from last year  federation says stores are discounting  turkeys  since more people will likely be trying  to cook on their own
 

